{"evt":"note_created","id":"note-mqg14209j5s","workspace":"/Users/imyu/Desktop/zonoid","title":"Cross-encoder rerank A/B on current KB: sharpens head (MRR, recall at 3) but regresses recall at 10","summary":"First A/B of the ORCH_RERANK /search cross-encoder against the live KB snapshot (28-query retrieval-bench, isolated snapshot daemon, ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6-v2). Measured 2026-06-15.\n\nOFF (structBoost only) → ON (cross-encoder + structBoost):\n- MRR@5: 0.7036 → 0.7262 (+0.023)\n- recall@3: 0.75 → 0.7857 (+0.036); precision@3: 0.25 → 0.2619\n- recall@10: 0.8214 → 0.7857 (REGRESSION −0.036); precision@10 0.0821 → 0.0786\n\nREADING: the cross-encoder improves the HEAD of the ranking (first relevant note surfaces higher — MRR up, recall@3 up) but HURTS tail recall@10. Two causes: (1) the cross-encoder occasionally ranks a genuinely-relevant note below a distractor; (2) the CE-2 design offsets the entire top-K=50 shortlist into [1,2] above the cosine tail, which FREEZES the reranked order — so recall is flat 0.7857 across k=3/5/10 instead of growing with k as the OFF arm does (0.75→0.79→0.82). The offset is too aggressive for tail recall.\n\nIMPLICATIONS: (a) the precision-over-recall thesis holds at the head even on the small 154-note KB; the premise is that this head benefit AMPLIFIES with distractor density, which the 100x-scale test (still pending) must confirm. (b) The recall@10 regression is a DESIGN signal: a softer blend (interpolate cosine+CE, or rerank-reorder-within-shortlist without the [1,2] offset) would likely preserve tail recall. (c) precision@k is a weak metric here because most eval cases have a single relevant note (P@5 capped at 0.2) — MRR and recall@k are the signals. DO NOT enable ORCH_RERANK in production on this evidence: the recall@10 regression is real and the threshold/blend is uncalibrated.","created_by":"main","valid_from":"2026-06-16T02:34:38.505Z","vec":[-0.07425793260335922,-0.07824430614709854,-0.03691410273313522,-0.014365658164024353